.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
.Dd September 6, 2026
.Dt SDWDATE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sdwdate
.Nd synchronize the clock through independent Tor onion services
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Op Fl -slew-only
.Op Fl -once
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
uses upstream sdwdate's three time-source pools, consensus checks and median
offset selection.
The network process runs as _sdwdate; a small root parent
accepts only clock corrections over an inherited private socket.
All time-source HTTP requests use local Tor SOCKS with remote name resolution.
Tor SAFECOOKIE control authentication must be configured;
see the package README.
.Pp
The first correction may step by at most 24 hours.
Later corrections advance
or retard the clock by at most 5 milliseconds per second, matching sclockadj.
Gradual corrections exceeding 30 seconds are rejected explicitly.
Intervals between completed queries are randomized between 60 and 180 minutes.
.Pp
.Fl -dry-run
validates requests without modifying the clock or the replay checkpoint.
.Fl -slew-only
forbids even the initial step.
.Fl -once
performs one cycle and exits, returning failure if it cannot obtain valid time.
.Pp
Do not run this daemon concurrently with
.Xr ntpd 8
or another clock writer.
The rc script refuses startup while ntpd is running.
The package never disables ntpd automatically.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ${SYSCONFDIR}/sdwdate.d/30_default.conf
Packaged sample containing unchanged upstream pools.
.It Pa ${SYSCONFDIR}/sdwdate.d/50_user.conf
Administrator overrides (not executed as shell code).
.It Pa /var/db/sdwdate
Replay checkpoint, owned by _sdwdate.
.It Pa /var/run/sdwdate/status
Atomic JSON status file, owned by _sdwdate.
.It Pa /var/run/sdwdate.lock
Root-owned single-instance lock.
Do not remove while the daemon runs.
.El
.Sh SIGNALS
SIGTERM and SIGINT stop the network process and cancel outstanding incremental
corrections.
A completed clock change cannot be rolled back automatically.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tor 1 ,
.Xr ntpd 8 ,
.Xr rcctl 8
.Sh CAVEATS
This is an initial
.Ox
adaptation requiring native privilege, Tor and clock-lifecycle testing.
A broken clock may prevent Tor bootstrapping or HTTPS
verification; no unauthenticated or clearnet fallback is provided.
